The screen pixmap's bits per pixel must be the screen's, not the depth's

This is the X server's fb library, not the kernel driver.

THE MISTAKE

    fb/fbpixmap.c, fbCreatePixmap():

        int bpp = BitsPerPixel(depth);
        paddedWidth = ((width * bpp + FB_MASK) >> FB_SHIFT) * sizeof(FbBits);

BitsPerPixel(depth) is how many bits a pixel VALUE occupies. The
screen's memory is laid out at the screen's bits per pixel, and for a
depth-24 visual in a 32-bit framebuffer those differ: the value is 24
bits and the storage is 32.

The pixmap's stride comes from PixmapBytePad(), which uses the
STORAGE's bytes per pixel - so the stride is right - while the pixmap's
bitsPerPixel came from the depth. The two disagreed, and everything the
fb library drew into the screen walked three bytes forward in a
four-byte row.

THE SYMPTOMS, and they are all one bug:

  * the X server's own dump of the root window said 24 bits per pixel
    where the framebuffer has 32, and its rows held 2560 pixels of
    data in a 1920-pixel screen;
  * the text was unreadable;
  * the mouse pointer was invisible;
  * anything translucent came out black, because the alpha byte was
    never written - which is why the XFCE logout menu was black and
    the window shadows were wrong.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES

  * the fb screen private remembers the screen's bits per pixel
    (fb/fb.h, fb/fbscreen.c);
  * fbCreatePixmap() uses it for the stride and for the pixmap's
    bitsPerPixel, and falls back to the depth's when the screen does
    not know yet;
  * the root pixmap's bits per pixel is the screen's too
    (mi/miscrinit.c).

AND WHAT IT DOES NOT DO - the honest part

This is not enough on its own, and the reason is worth recording:
the fb library also FETCHES and STORES pixel values, and those
conversions are keyed on the DEPTH. A 24-bit pixel value has to be
placed in a 32-bit slot with its alpha set, and the code that does
that (the 24-to-32 conversion) is in a MODULE that this version of the
X server does not have - upstream calls it fb24_32, and the modesetting
driver uses it for exactly this case. The fbdev driver does not.

So with this patch alone the screen goes black: the fills write 24-bit
values into 32-bit slots without the alpha, and the result is
translucent black.

The complete fix is the fb24_32 conversion, in the fb library or as a
module, which is what upstream does.

WHAT WAS PROVEN, before the rollback

  * the patch builds (the X server is built on the box; it is aarch64
    and the build machine is not);
  * with it, the X server's own dump of the root window reports 32
    bits per pixel and a 7680-byte stride - which matches the
    framebuffer exactly, and did not before;
  * the comparison against the framebuffer went from 272 differing
    pixels to 103, and all 103 are the mouse pointer, which is a
    software-cursor overlay and so is not part of the root window at
    all.

The box was rolled back to the stock X server: the desktop works
there, and the remaining defect is the one this patch does not finish.
